  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Find the product $EF$, if\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)\\end{aligned}].$\n\n| $EF=$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $E$ is the $4$th *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $4\\times 4$ matrix $F$ by the $4$th *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{4}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $4$th *row* of $F.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nEF=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EF & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & -8 & \\sqrt{5} & 14 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 9 \\\\ \\sqrt{17} & -6 & 0 & \\frac{e}{3} \\\\ -11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & \\sqrt{19} & -4 & -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
